   "slug": "i-cant-solve-this-but-i-can-see-you",
   "title": "I can\u2019t solve this, but i can see you",
   "excerpt": "I Can\u2019t Fix Your Flood, but I Can Listen Differently \u2014 And Hope You\u2019ll Do the Same",
   "publishedAt": "2024-11-29T09:00:03Z",
   "category": "personal",
   "tags": [
    "series:top-of-my-mind"
   ],
   "sourceName": "Medium",
   "sourceUrl": "https://medium.com/the-top-of-my-mind/i-cant-solve-this-but-i-can-see-you-9f6a511af8cf",
   "wordCount": 829,
   "bodyText": "I can\u2019t solve it, but I see you\n\n\u201cHearing the Flood: Learning to Listen to What Others Carry\u201d\n\n\u2014 \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 -\n\nI\u2019ve spent a lot of time writing about what it\u2019s like to live in my own mental flood \u2014 the constant overthinking, the weight of masking, and the marathon of just trying to exist in a world that doesn\u2019t always feel built for me. But if there\u2019s one thing I\u2019ve learned through this process, it\u2019s that I can\u2019t solve anyone else\u2019s flood. I can\u2019t save them from their struggle or give them a roadmap out of it. I wish I could. But what I can do is listen differently now. I can hear someone else\u2019s experience in a way that I wasn\u2019t capable of before \u2014 not because I have all the answers, but because I know what it feels like to carry something invisible and heavy.\n\nThe truth is, no two floods are the same. What overwhelms me might be a steady flow for someone else, and what feels insurmountable to someone else might be a current I\u2019ve already learned to navigate. But that doesn\u2019t make anyone\u2019s experience less valid or less worthy of understanding. It just means we\u2019re all doing the best we can with what we\u2019ve been handed \u2014 trying to swim in waters that feel deeper or colder than they should.\n\nI Can\u2019t Solve You, but I Can See You\n\nWhen I was deep in my own spiral, the last thing I needed was someone trying to \u201cfix\u201d me. Well-meaning advice or platitudes only made me feel more isolated, like my inability to take their suggestions was just further proof that I was broken. What I needed wasn\u2019t a solution \u2014 it was to feel understood, to know that someone could sit with me in the mess of it without trying to clean it up.\n\nThat\u2019s what I try to offer now: presence without pressure. I\u2019ve learned that I don\u2019t have to fix someone else\u2019s flood to make a difference. I don\u2019t have to wade into their waters and try to pull them out. All I have to do is let them know I see them. That I\u2019m here. That their experience matters, even if it\u2019s messy, even if I can\u2019t fully understand it.\n\nListening Without Solving\n\nHearing someone differently means letting go of the instinct to respond, to advise, to fix. It means making space for their story without needing to center myself in it. It means resisting the urge to compare struggles or offer quick solutions, and instead just letting their words sit between us, as they are.\n\nThis is harder than it sounds, especially when you care about someone and want to ease their burden. But I\u2019ve come to understand that the best thing I can do isn\u2019t to lighten their load \u2014 it\u2019s to validate it. To let them feel seen, without judgment or expectation. And sometimes, that\u2019s enough.\n\nHoping for Reciprocation\n\nAt the same time, I\u2019ve started to hope that others can meet me in the same way. That they, too, can listen without trying to fix me. That they can hear what I\u2019m saying \u2014 really hear it \u2014 without feeling the need to tell me it\u2019s going to be okay, or that I\u2019ll figure it out, or that it could always be worse.\n\nWhat I hope for is mutual recognition: a willingness to sit in the discomfort of someone else\u2019s experience without needing to smooth it over. To create a space where vulnerability isn\u2019t met with solutions but with understanding. I think that\u2019s what so many of us are looking for \u2014 not answers, but connection. Not someone to save us, but someone to remind us that we\u2019re not alone.\n\nThe Power of Being Heard\n\nThere\u2019s something profound about being heard \u2014 truly heard. It\u2019s not a solution, and it doesn\u2019t make the flood go away. But it shifts something. It reminds you that you\u2019re not invisible, that your experience isn\u2019t insignificant. And that can be enough to keep you going, to keep you treading water for one more day.\n\nI don\u2019t claim to have this all figured out. I\u2019m still learning how to listen, how to hold space for others while carrying my own weight. But if there\u2019s one thing I\u2019ve gained from my own experience, it\u2019s this: we don\u2019t need to save each other to make an impact. Sometimes, the most powerful thing we can do is simply acknowledge the flood \u2014 ours, theirs, all of it \u2014 and keep showing up, even when the waters feel too deep.\n\nI don\u2019t know what your flood looks like. I don\u2019t know how you navigate it or what keeps you afloat. But I see you. I hear you. And I hope, in some small way, that we can learn to carry these storms together \u2014 not by solving them, but by honoring them, and by reminding each other that we\u2019re not alone in the fight.",
