   "sourceName": "Substack",
   "sourceUrl": "https://stevepeeleii.substack.com/p/i-spent-a-week-automating-the-most",
   "wordCount": 310,
   "bodyText": "There's a specific kind of tired that hits at 1am when the input list finally arrives and you open PowerPoint.\n\nYou know the routine. Rectangles for the drums. Smaller rectangles for the wedges. Labels in 10pt font. Export a PDF that looks like it was made by someone who gave up halfway through \u2014 because you did.\n\nI've done this hundreds of times. And at some point this week I thought: why? What's actually hard about this?\n\nThe mic choices aren't hard. Kick gets a Beta 52. Snare gets a 57. It's the same answer 90% of the time \u2014 the only real thinking is the weird 10%, and even that's just pattern matching.\n\nThe stage layout isn't hard. Drums back center, vocals up front, everyone knows the vocabulary.\n\nThe channel sheet isn't hard. It's formatting. Number, instrument, mic, stand, notes.\n\nNone of it is hard. It's just tedious. So I built StagePlot AI to do the tedious part.\n\nYou paste the input list. It handles the defaults, draws the plot, formats the sheet. If you disagree with a mic choice, you override it \u2014 \"gtr1 e609\" \u2014 because your judgment still matters. The tool just stops making you retype the obvious stuff.\n\nI'm putting this out there to see if it's useful to anyone besides me. It's free to try. If engineers actually use it, I'll keep building \u2014 saved bands, real PDF export, the stuff that makes it a daily driver. If not, I learned something.\n\nMy broader thinking: there's a whole category of live sound workflow that's just... un-automated tedium. Advance sheets, input lists, stage plots. Nobody's building for this because the market looks small. But small markets with real pain are fine by me.\n\nTry it if it makes sense: https://stageplot-ai-stevepeeles-projects.vercel.app/\n\nBuilt by SPIIX \u2014 the operating system for demand. See what I'm about at signalsbyspii.com.",
